{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`list_tickets` against this store can terminate with `MCP request failed: Transport closed`\nand no structured error payload — the caller cannot tell whether the query was invalid, the\nserver failed, or the response was simply too large.\n\n## Proven root cause\n\nUnfiltered `tools/call list_tickets {}` against the deployed endpoint returned `http=200`,\n**94,541,551 bytes over 177.29 seconds**, in one well-formed SSE frame. **The server does not\nfail.** The client abandons a 94.5 MB / 177 s response, and how it abandons is client-specific\n(Copilot CLI: `Transport closed`; a 30 s-budget client: timeout; a third: 219,815 chars spilled\nto disk). None can distinguish \"too big\" from \"server died\", because the server offers no bound\nand no structured signal. Full measurement tables are in the Phase 1 and Phase 4 comments.\n\nCandidates closed: BigInt serialization **REJECTED** (the call completes and parses; that is\nsibling `unreal-milky-sloth` / `6fe7-956f-4901-45cf`, a different mechanism — a type error, not\na size one — and this measurement is post-fix); deploy cutover **REJECTED** (reproducible three\ndays after the 18:54:29Z replacement, from a session never orphaned).\n\n## Scope: this ticket is the BOUND. The projection is its prerequisite.\n\nThe fix has two independent halves, and they are two changes.\n\n- **`resistant-constant-nurseshark` (`98b8-5f08-1569-45cc`) — the lean projection.** Widens the\n  lean discovery row to drop the signature material (88% of a \"lean\" list's bytes), shared\n  across both read backends. Linked `depends_on`. **It must land first**: without it, this\n  bound would refuse even `list_tickets(status=\"open\")` at 224,610 lean bytes.\n- **This ticket — the bound.** A response-size limit and a structured refusal.\n\n## The guard that already exists, and why it is not enough\n\nNot greenfield. `_cap_workflow_payload` (`_mcp_budget.py`, extracted here from\n`mcp_server.py`) states the contract — *\"under the client's ~25K-token budget (WS-ffc4)\"*,\n90,000 bytes — but is wired only to `get_workflow_status` / `get_workflow_result`.\n`list_tickets` and `ready_tickets` have **no size check at all**.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Cause identified and explicitly reconciled with both sibling tickets (above).\n\n- [ ] **Bounded, measured at a named seam.** Both list tools bound at the same 90,000-byte\n      `_LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET_BYTES` (aliasing `_WORKFLOW_TOKEN_BUDGET_BYTES`). Raw reducer rows\n      UNDER-estimate the real payload — the dangerous direction, because an under-estimating\n      bound passes something that still overruns — so the bound runs INSIDE the tool body after\n      `TicketStateOut.model_validate` and calls `js_safe_result` on those rows itself, rather\n      than waiting for the error guard, which applies that transform only after the body\n      returns. Same pure function, same rows: measured bytes equal emitted bytes, while the\n      raise point stays where a structured error is still deliverable. Measured at 2,855 rows,\n      raw 552,772 -> post-validate 1,526,327 -> post-`js_safe_result` 1,537,747, a 178%\n      under-estimate; the dominant axis is `TicketStateOut`'s DECLARED defaults re-adding every\n      field the lean projection dropped, not the 19-digit-int quoting.\n\n- [ ] **Structured refusal, right exception TYPE, closed vocabulary.** Raises a\n      `rebar._errors.RebarError` — the type matters, not just the attribute: `_envelope_error`\n      only envelopes a known rebar exception type, so a plain `Exception` carrying `.error_code`\n      is dropped and the caller gets an unstructured failure. Carries\n      `error_code=\"response_too_large\"`, naming match count, byte size, budget and a remedy.\n      Never a partial list; never a bare transport close. The value is added to\n      `rebar.KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` and `tests/unit/api_surface_baseline.json`. `error_code_for`\n      needs no change (step 5 returns a non-empty `exc.error_code`); no schema change\n      (`error_envelope.error` is an open string, not an enum).\n\n- [ ] **REFUSE, do not truncate.** Deliberately NOT the truncate-and-flag treatment a workflow\n      payload gets: a shortened list is indistinguishable from a complete one, which is the\n      vacuous result this project refuses.\n\n- [ ] **The remedy must be one the REFUSED TOOL can execute.** `list_tickets` accepts six\n      filters and names them. **`ready_tickets` accepts NO filter argument** — only `sort` and\n      the `full` its prerequisite adds, and `full` switches SHAPE, not which tickets come back\n      — so a shared \"narrow with has_tag\" message would name arguments the tool rejects.\n      `ready_tickets`' refusal points at `next_batch(epic_id)` instead. Its measured headroom\n      after the projection is ~1.7% (59 rows, 88,480 of 90,000 bytes; see comment), so refusing\n      is a near-term state and the remedy has to work. The budget is deliberately NOT raised:\n      it is the client's real constraint, and raising it moves the failure back into the client\n      where there is no structured signal.\n\n- [ ] **The extraction is along an existing call-graph seam.** The budget cluster moves to\n      `src/rebar/_mcp_budget.py` because both bounds already measure through `_payload_bytes`\n      and nothing else in the server calls them; `mcp_server.py` is at 797 of its 800-LOC cap\n      and adding the list bound in place would breach it. Verified by\n      `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py`.\n\n- [ ] **Regression coverage asserts the error's CONTENTS**, not merely that a refusal occurred.\n      The envelope is `{error, input, message, exit_code}`, so `error` is asserted as its own\n      key while match count, byte size, budget and remedy are asserted as substrings WITHIN\n      `message`. Asserted for BOTH tools, with the per-tool remedy checked separately\n      (`list_tickets` names its filters; `ready_tickets` names `next_batch` and must NOT name\n      filters it lacks). Plus an under-budget list returned whole, and the regression window —\n      250 plain rows whose RAW size is 49,652 (under budget) while the wire payload is 135,902\n      (51% over) must still be refused. Driven through the REGISTERED `CallToolRequest` handler\n      a real client reaches, so a guard applied only to the post-`build_server()` instance\n      cannot pass. Mutation-checked: neutering the budget comparison must turn these RED.\n\n- [ ] **Rollout.** The refusal is ADDITIVE — a previously-unbounded call gains a structured\n      failure it never had, and nothing that fits today starts failing — so it is recorded in\n      `docs/release-notes.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` as a new behaviour, not a breaking change.\n      (The narrowing half's breaking-change notes belong to the prerequisite story.)\n\n- [ ] **LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE.** `list_tickets` and `ready_tickets` through the\n      deployed endpoint return results (or a structured limit error) with no transport close.\n      **Auth precondition:** the endpoint is behind the OAuth gate, so the client MUST present a\n      valid bearer token; a `401`/`403` is a FAILED validation, not an environment excuse.\n      Evidence must be OBSERVED against the deployed server and recorded in a comment naming the\n      tools, arguments and actual response. A passing local test does NOT satisfy this, and\n      neither does a merged change: the container must have redeployed and served the call.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- `make lint` (ruff + format-check + complexity ratchet `new=0 increased=0`), `make typecheck`,\n  `pytest tests/unit tests/interfaces` green.\n- Change pushed to Gerrit, `LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`, stacked on the prerequisite.\n- Live validation recorded as a comment."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787959303909455952,"uuid":"8b0a6097-e2c0-4e95-9c3f-fde9e31130eb"}